  <h1>Driving the HFT Lab</h1>
  <p class="why"><b>Why this matters:</b> lessons 0001–0005 each taught one technique. This one is the
  <b>map</b> — how the whole repo fits together, what to run, and how each folder is a rung on a ladder
  from a daily lead-lag study up to a nanosecond order book. Read this first when you come back cold.</p>

  <div class="bus"></div>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <h2><span class="n">01</span> The one idea: two labs, one ladder</h2>

  <p class="lead">This repo is not one program. It is <b>two research labs stacked on top of each other</b>,
  joined by a compiled bridge. Both ask the same kind of question — <em>“does one thing lead another, and
  what does it cost to trade on it?”</em> — but at timescales a billion times apart.</p>

  <div class="layers">
    <div class="layer daily">
      <span class="tag">Layer 1 · Daily research (Python)</span>
      <div><code>src/oil_alpha/</code> — the beginner-friendly starter kit the README describes.</div>
      <div class="flow">
        download <span class="arw">→</span> clean <span class="arw">→</span> analyze <span class="arw">→</span> cost
        <br><span class="sub">yfinance/FRED · Apr-2020 &amp; roll-day traps · CCF/Granger/cointegration · budget + bp</span>
      </div>
      <div class="muted" style="margin-top:10px;font-size:13.5px">Data is <b>free daily closes</b>. The headline
      result: <b>WTI futures (CL) lead the energy ETF (XLE)</b> at short lags. No order book needed.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="layer hft">
      <span class="tag">Layer 2 · Intraday microstructure (C++ ⇄ Python)</span>
      <div><code>cpp/itch/</code> + <code>src/oil_alpha/microstructure/</code> — the real HFT machinery.</div>
      <div class="flow">
        ITCH bytes <span class="arw">→</span> order book <span class="arw">→</span> signals <span class="arw">→</span> sim / replay
        <br><span class="sub">parse frames · reconstruct book · OFI/microprice/Kyle-λ · replay clock · market-making · VHDL</span>
      </div>
      <div class="muted" style="margin-top:10px;font-size:13.5px">Data is a <b>real Nasdaq tick feed</b>
      (the AAPL fixture). This is where you meet queues, latency, and hardware.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>The README calls Layer 2 <em>“out of scope (future milestones)”</em> — that page was written for the daily
  starter kit. Everything under <code>cpp/</code>, <code>hw/</code>, and <code>microstructure/</code> is that future,
  now built. The learning materials (<code>docs/learning/</code>) and perf write-ups (<code>docs/perf/</code>)
  are the third thing: the <b>lab notebook</b> that turns each build into a lesson.</p>

  <div class="progress-controls"><button class="mark" data-mark>◻ mark section read</button></div>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <h2><span class="n">02</span> The knowledge: what each layer actually does</h2>

  <h3>Layer 1 — the daily study is one generator</h3>
  <p>Everything in the daily lab flows through <b>one function</b>: <code>run_pipeline()</code> in
  <code>src/oil_alpha/pipeline.py</code>. It is a <b>generator</b> — it <code>yield</code>s a
  <code>ProgressEvent</code> after each stage instead of returning once at the end. That single design choice is
  why the Streamlit GUI and the headless CLI can never disagree: <b>they consume the exact same event stream.</b></p>

  <h3>Layer 2 — the C++ core is imported as one module</h3>
  <p>The C++ under <code>cpp/itch/</code> is compiled into a Python extension called <code>signals_cpp</code>
  (via pybind11 — that’s lesson 0001). Your Python analysis code does <code>import signals_cpp</code>, calls one
  function, and gets back <b>columnar arrays</b> (one list per field) ready to drop into a DataFrame. The heavy
  parsing runs at C++ speed; the plotting and stats stay in comfortable Python.</p>

  <h3>The lab notebook — perf docs and lessons</h3>
  <p><code>docs/perf/NN-*.md</code> records a <b>measured</b> claim after each build (a number, not a vibe).
  <code>docs/learning/</code> turns the non-obvious concept behind that build into a lesson + drill + vocab.
  <code>PROGRESS.md</code> is the running index of what’s been taught; <code>GLOSSARY.md</code> the shared vocabulary.</p>

  <div class="note"><b>Mental hook:</b> Layer 1 answers <em>“is there an edge?”</em> with cheap daily data.
  Layer 2 answers <em>“could you actually capture it?”</em> once queues, latency, and impact are real.
  The gap between those two answers <em>is</em> high-frequency trading.</div>

  <div class="progress-controls"><button class="mark" data-mark>◻ mark section read</button></div>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <h2><span class="n">03</span> Minimal example: the generator spine</h2>
  <p>Here is the pattern that runs the whole daily lab, stripped to its skeleton. Understand this and you
  understand how to <em>drive</em> Layer 1 from either the GUI or a script.</p>

<pre><span class="c"># The producer: one generator, yields once per stage (never returns early).</span>
<span class="k">def</span> <span class="f">run_pipeline</span>(cfg=<span class="k">None</span>):
    stages = [<span class="s">"download"</span>, <span class="s">"clean"</span>, <span class="s">"lead-lag"</span>, <span class="s">"cointegration"</span>, <span class="s">"costs"</span>]
    <span class="k">for</span> i, stage <span class="k">in</span> <span class="f">enumerate</span>(stages):
        <span class="k">yield</span> ProgressEvent(stage, <span class="s">"start"</span>, pct=i/<span class="f">len</span>(stages))   <span class="c"># tell the UI we began</span>
        result = <span class="f">do_the_work</span>(stage)                                <span class="c"># the actual stats</span>
        <span class="k">yield</span> ProgressEvent(stage, <span class="s">"ok"</span>, pct=(i+<span class="n">1</span>)/<span class="f">len</span>(stages)) <span class="c"># tell the UI we finished</span>
    <span class="k">yield</span> ProgressEvent(<span class="s">"done"</span>, <span class="s">"done"</span>, pct=<span class="n">1.0</span>, payload=result) <span class="c"># last event carries everything</span>

<span class="c"># Consumer A — a headless script (what `python -m oil_alpha.pipeline` does):</span>
<span class="k">for</span> ev <span class="k">in</span> <span class="f">run_pipeline</span>():
    <span class="f">print</span>(<span class="s">f"[{ev.pct:.0%}] {ev.stage}: {ev.status}"</span>)
    <span class="k">if</span> ev.stage == <span class="s">"done"</span>:
        summary = ev.payload            <span class="c"># the full PipelineResult</span>

<span class="c"># Consumer B — the Streamlit dashboard (app/dashboard.py) walks the SAME loop,</span>
<span class="c"># but paints a progress bar per event instead of printing. One source of truth.</span></pre>

  <p class="muted">The real <code>run_pipeline</code> yields richer events (<code>warn</code>/<code>error</code>
  statuses, per-stage payloads, an <code>APIBudget</code>, and <code>ComputeMetrics</code> timings), but the shape
  is exactly this: <b>produce a stream, let any consumer drive it.</b></p>

