  <h2><span class="n">05</span> Often confused with each other</h2>
  <p>The single biggest source of confusion in this repo is thinking the two “lead-lag” studies are the same
  thing. They share a <em>question</em> but almost nothing else.</p>

  <div class="vs">
    <div class="col a">
      <h4>Daily lead-lag (Layer 1)</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Data:</b> free daily closes from yfinance/FRED — one row per day.</li>
        <li><b>Question:</b> does WTI (CL) lead the energy ETF (XLE) day-to-day?</li>
        <li><b>Tools:</b> CCF, Granger causality, cointegration on price levels.</li>
        <li><b>Trap:</b> the <b>Epps effect</b> — coarse bars make everything look contemporaneous.</li>
        <li><b>Lives in:</b> <code>analysis/leadlag.py</code>, pure Python, no order book.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="col b">
      <h4>Intraday microstructure lead-lag (Layer 2)</h4>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Data:</b> a real Nasdaq ITCH tick feed — millions of events per day.</li>
        <li><b>Question:</b> does order-flow imbalance move the microprice next?</li>
        <li><b>Tools:</b> a reconstructed book, OFI, Kyle’s λ, Hayashi-Yoshida on ticks.</li>
        <li><b>Trap:</b> <b>look-ahead / queue fantasy</b> — assuming you’d have been filled.</li>
        <li><b>Lives in:</b> <code>cpp/itch/</code> + <code>microstructure/</code>, C++ speed + Python stats.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p class="muted">They <b>share</b>: the “does X lead Y, and is it tradable after costs” shape, and the CCF
  sign convention (peak lag <code>k&gt;0</code> ⇒ x leads y). They <b>diverge</b> on data granularity, the
  dominant bias to fear, and whether an order book even exists. <b>Use Layer 1 to decide if an edge is worth
  chasing; use Layer 2 to find out if microstructure would let you keep it.</b></p>

